<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<section id="S4.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.1 </span>Ablation On Training Steps</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigated our model’s performance across a spectrum of Vision-Language Model (VLM) fine-tuning, from zero fine-tuning to excessive fine-tuning that resulted in a significant degradation of language abilities in Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.5.1 Ablation On Training Steps ‣ 4.5 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridge Thinking and Acting: Unleashing Physical Potential of VLM with Generalizable Action Expert" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. We measured the success rate on a subset of our RoboTwin tasks with two approaches: 1) directly using Inverse Kinematics (IK) to execute trajectories generated by the VLM, and 2) utilizing our generalizable action expert to process the VLM’s output. Concurrently, we benchmarked the VLM’s language capabilities (e.g., using MMLU). The results clearly indicate that employing our generalizable action expert allows the model’s performance to saturate much more rapidly, as clearly demostrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3 Ablation On Different Training Strategy ‣ 4.5 Ablation Studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Bridge Thinking and Acting: Unleashing Physical Potential of VLM with Generalizable Action Expert" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. This significantly reduces the required number of Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) steps for the VLM, thereby preserving its crucial language-based generalization capabilities.</p>
</div>
